    {/* Atlas — 2D semantic browser */}
    <section id="atlas" class="border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]/60 p-5 space-y-3 scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)]">
        ▸ Atlas (2D semantic browser)
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        The <a href="/atlas" class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)] underline decoration-[color:var(--color-border-bright)] hover:decoration-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">/atlas</a>
        page is a 2D projection of the corpus's 1024-dim
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">voyage-3</code>
        embeddings. We use
        <a href="https://umap-learn.readthedocs.io/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)] underline decoration-[color:var(--color-border-bright)] hover:decoration-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">UMAP</a>
        with <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">n_components=2</code>,
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">n_neighbors=15</code>,
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">min_dist=0.1</code>, and
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">random_state=42</code>.
        The projection runs at build time via
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">scripts/build_atlas_layout.py</code>;
        the browser loads a 343 KB static asset (rounded-coordinate JSON)
        and renders it through
        <a href="https://github.com/flekschas/regl-scatterplot" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)] underline decoration-[color:var(--color-border-bright)] hover:decoration-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">regl-scatterplot</a> (WebGL).
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        Why UMAP and not t-SNE: at 4k+ points, UMAP preserves global
        topology better — clusters that are far apart in 1024-dim land
        far apart in 2D. t-SNE produces visually striking but
        navigation-misleading layouts where global distances are
        compressed away. PCA was a fallback option but lost the
        non-linear structure we wanted to surface.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        Why 2D and not 3D: pan + zoom on a plane is muscle memory; 3D
        rotation is novelty. The atlas is meant to be a
        <em>navigation surface</em>, not a showpiece. Mobile especially
        benefits — at viewport widths below 400px we drop the canvas
        entirely and render a k-means cluster list instead.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">Honest framing.</strong>
        UMAP is a low-dim approximation; the cluster boundaries you see
        are not curated topic labels. The layout is reproducible only
        because we pin the seed — re-running unseeded would shuffle the
        coordinates without changing the underlying semantics. Color is
        by reporting agency (Department of War / FBI / NASA / Department
        of State), which is metadata-derived, not learned. Treat the
        atlas as a <em>lens</em>, not a ground truth.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* Reproducibility */}
    <section id="reproducibility" class="border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]/60 p-5 space-y-3 scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)]">
        ▸ Reproducibility
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        The full pipeline lives at
        <a href="https://github.com/BPSAI/pursue-index" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)] underline decoration-[color:var(--color-border-bright)] hover:decoration-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">github.com/BPSAI/pursue-index</a>.
        The manifest is version-controlled in the repo, hash-pinned to the
        upstream CSV. From a clean clone with the upstream CSV available,
        any reader can:
      </p>
      <pre class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg-elevated)] border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-3 font-mono text-[11px] text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)] overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre"><code>{`pursue scrape run    # writes manifests/latest.json + archives raw CSV
pursue download run  # fetches PDFs/IMGs idempotently
pursue ocr run       # writes pages.jsonl per card
pursue embed run     # writes the in-browser embeddings payload`}</code></pre>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        Every stage is rerunnable on the same manifest as a no-op. Every
        stage's output is content-addressed by
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">card_id</code>,
